<header class="ref-head"><span class="hero-ornament"></span><h1 class="research-title">13 April in history</h1><p class="ref-lede">6 events that happened on 13 April, from 1111 to 1943.</p></header>
<ol class="day-events"><li class="tih-event-card"><p class="tih-event-year">1111</p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Pope Paschal II crowns Henry V emperor under duress</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Having held the Pope captive for two months, Henry V forced him to concede the right of investiture and crown him in Rome. The concession was soon revoked, and the dispute lasted until 1122.</p></li><li class="tih-event-card"><p class="tih-event-year">1598</p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Henry IV signs the Edict of Nantes</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Henry IV granted French Protestants freedom of worship in specified places, civil rights and fortified towns. The edict ended the Wars of Religion until Louis XIV revoked it in 1685.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag tih-tag-profile" href="/empire/dutch-empire">Dutch Empire</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv">Louis XIV</a></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><p class="tih-event-year">1742</p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Handel's Messiah has its premiere in Dublin</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Handel's oratorio was first performed at the New Music Hall in Fishamble Street, Dublin, in aid of charity. It became one of the most performed choral works in the Western tradition.</p></li><li class="tih-event-card"><p class="tih-event-year">1829</p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Catholic Relief Act receives royal assent</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">The Roman Catholic Relief Act allowed Catholics to sit in Parliament and hold most public offices. It was forced through by Wellington and Peel after Daniel O'Connell's election in County Clare.</p></li><li class="tih-event-card"><p class="tih-event-year">1919</p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Troops fire on a crowd at Jallianwala Bagh, Amritsar</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Soldiers under Brigadier-General Reginald Dyer fired without warning into an unarmed crowd in an enclosed garden. At least 379 were killed by official count; the massacre hardened Indian opposition to British rule.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amritsar">Amritsar</a></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><p class="tih-event-year">1943</p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Germany announces discovery of the Katyń graves</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Berlin radio reported mass graves of Polish officers in the Katyń Forest near Smolensk. They had been shot by the Soviet NKVD in 1940, a crime Moscow denied until 1990.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smolensk">Smolensk</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a></div></li></ol>
